00:21:46.810 --> 00:21:49.539
Tanner, Bertrand: So just to illustrate again.

106
00:21:49.550 --> 00:22:06.910
Tanner, Bertrand: that process that I went through, we've got calcium binding, then you get exposing the thin filament binding sites, and when myosin binds to actin, it's in an ADP and phosphate state, so after it's hydrolyzed the ATP, it's got those nucleotide products.

107
00:22:06.910 --> 00:22:17.479
Tanner, Bertrand: And then as it releases phosphate, that's when you get the power stroke that generates force, and then as ADP is released, ATP comes on, and that leads to dissociation.

