00:23:31.750 --> 00:23:41.139
Tanner, Bertrand: with how the cross bridge goes through its cycle as you change the muscle length. And I've got one little example, we'll go over that today, but part of why I started thinking about this

117
00:23:41.260 --> 00:23:52.819
Tanner, Bertrand: had to do with the Frank-Starling relationship, which that in the heart means if you get greater filling of the ventricle, the heart's gonna contract more forcefully and faster to eject more blood.

118
00:23:52.930 --> 00:24:03.419
Tanner, Bertrand: As either your heart rate increases, or you get, just greater, greater filling, you need to match that greater ejection. And so.

119
00:24:03.420 --> 00:24:13.940
Tanner, Bertrand: The Frank-Sarling law at the organ level suggests that cardiac output increases as that end diastolic volume, or that filling of the ventricle increases, and

120
00:24:13.940 --> 00:24:31.079
Tanner, Bertrand: Normally, this is a relatively steep relationship, as shown in the black line, but the dashed line is… it's… this relationship is suppressed, and that occurs with disease or dysfunction and heart failure. And sort of at the molecular and cellular level.

121
00:24:31.080 --> 00:24:42.260
Tanner, Bertrand: An analogous finding, or an analogous characteristic of muscle, is an increase in force and calcium sensitivity as you increase the muscle length. And so.

122
00:24:42.260 --> 00:24:58.129
Tanner, Bertrand: If you think about force as a function of calcium, if you look at the log of calcium, as you increase calcium, you'll get an increase in force with this sigmoidal relationship, and that is greater maximum force and an increase in calcium sensitivity

123
00:24:58.590 --> 00:25:03.930
