126
00:25:27.430 --> 00:25:44.530
Tanner, Bertrand: Okay, so this next little example is looking at RAT myocardial mechanics, but this starts to get into some of the system analysis methods, where we're using, in this particular case, sinusoidal length perturbation analysis to probe the crossbridge kinetics.

127
00:25:44.690 --> 00:25:55.789
Tanner, Bertrand: And so this is a system analysis method that is called single input, single output analysis, where the muscle acts as a viscoelastic system, and if you

128
00:25:55.790 --> 00:26:07.970
Tanner, Bertrand: wiggle the muscle like you put a sinusoidal input into it by changing the length. That's going to have some sort of effect on the force or the stress, which is force per cross-sectional area for the system output.

129
00:26:08.230 --> 00:26:13.220
Tanner, Bertrand: And so, if we think of strain as a function of time, shown here in black.

130
00:26:13.850 --> 00:26:28.919
Tanner, Bertrand: You'll get some response for the stress, for the stress response as a function of time, and it's a… also a sine wave, because a lot of those system characteristics are preserved, but what's different is you get sort of some shift in time

131
00:26:29.060 --> 00:26:42.099
Tanner, Bertrand: And this phase difference, or this shift in time between the input and the output, that changes as a function of frequency. So you'll get more or less shift in time as how this behaves. And that shift

132
00:26:42.300 --> 00:26:52.690
Tanner, Bertrand: As a function of frequency, allows you to characterize or estimate some of what's going on with the cross-bridge cycling properties, or the cross-bridge kinetics.

133
00:26:53.540 --> 00:27:07.459
Tanner, Bertrand: And so, the way we break this out is we turn this into an elastic and a viscous modulus. So the elastic component, when the muscle's acting like a spring, or the viscous component, when the muscle's acting like a damper.

134
00:27:07.700 --> 00:27:12.820
Tanner, Bertrand: But you get a viscoelastic response as a summation of those in total.

135
00:27:12.970 --> 00:27:29.860
Tanner, Bertrand: And so, you can break this up where the elastic response is directly in phase with the strain input, with the… this is the big dashed line, and that's some sort of elastic element, and then the amplitude related to the autophase component, or the…

136
00:27:30.210 --> 00:27:37.309
Tanner, Bertrand: Sine wave would be… the… the viscous component.

137
00:27:37.950 --> 00:27:47.569
Tanner, Bertrand: And so we can look at those characteristics and see how they change as a function of frequency to estimate cross-bridge cycling. And just to be…

138
00:27:47.770 --> 00:28:03.119
Tanner, Bertrand: clear… the actual data that I'm showing you is coming from stochastic length perturbation analysis, where it's a sum of a bunch of sine waves, but that's just, like, a subtle detail. The principle is the same, it's just the data looks a little bit different.

139
00:28:03.440 --> 00:28:09.320
Tanner, Bertrand: If you were actually looking at sinusoidal data versus, the Gaussian white noise data.

